\clearpage
\section{Studies new in the 3.5.0 release}
\label{sec:new350}

The 3.5.0 release adds a number of new methods (robust inference,
influence diagnostics, a \textsc{ransac} initial estimator, Mallows
design weights and structured random-effect covariances; see
\code{vignette("robustlmm-3.5.0")}). Each is backed by one or more
simulation studies. The scripts are available in the package sources
on GitHub and are linked below; the worked,
\Sexpr{}-reproducible headline numbers appear in the
\code{robustlmm-3.5.0} vignette. This section describes each study's
design and conclusions. Because the full result objects exceed the
CRAN size limit, the figures are not rebuilt here; install the
\code{full-results} version (Section~\ref{sec:thecode}) to regenerate
them.

\subsection{Inference: coverage, size and power}
\label{sec:inferenceStudy}

\script{inferenceStudy.R} evaluates the finite-sample behaviour
of the new inference methods (the cluster-sandwich \code{vcov}, the
Wald and bootstrap \code{confint}, and the robust \code{anova}). It
sweeps the number of groups $J$ and several error- and
random-effect-contamination strategies, and records, for each method:
the empirical coverage of the nominal $95\%$ confidence intervals, the
Type-I error of the tests at nominal $5\%$, and the power under a
fixed-effect alternative.

\emph{Conclusions.} On clean Gaussian data all methods are
well-calibrated. Under error-distribution contamination the robust
Wald intervals are markedly shorter than the classical ones at matched
coverage, and the robust Wald \code{anova} is more powerful than the
classical likelihood-ratio test while holding its size. The
cluster-sandwich covariance is a large-$J$ device: at small $J$ it
under-covers and the sandwich-based tests become anti-conservative,
returning to nominal only for larger $J$; \code{vcov_sandwich} warns
below $20$ clusters, and the conclusions are distilled into the
\code{?vcov\_sandwich} and \code{?anova.rlmerMod} caveats. The
variance-component bootstrap test (\code{anova(f0, f1, test =
  "boot"})) calibrates correctly under error contamination but is
anti-conservative when whole groups are contaminated, which motivates
the cluster-level influence diagnostics below.

\subsection{Leverage breakdown and gross-error sensitivity}
\label{sec:leverageBreakdown}

The $\psi$-functions of the RSE bound the influence of outliers in the
response but not of high-leverage design points.
\script{leverageBreakdown.R} contaminates a fraction of the
observations by moving them onto a spurious regression line at a large
robust distance in covariate space, and compares the slope bias of
\code{lmer}, the plain RSE and the Mallows-weighted RSE
(\code{design.weights = "mcd"}) as the contamination fraction grows.
\script{leverageGES.R} complements it by tracing the
gross-error sensitivity --- the supremum of the (standardised)
influence as a single design point is moved out along a covariate.

\emph{Conclusions.} The plain RSE breaks down with \code{lmer} under
leverage contamination (good-leverage outliers have small residuals,
so the $\psi$-functions do not down-weight them), whereas the
Mallows-weighted fit stays close to the clean-data estimate, with
several-fold smaller slope bias at moderate contamination. The
gross-error sensitivity of the plain RSE is unbounded in the leverage
direction, while the Mallows weights make it bounded.

\subsection{Efficiency cost of the Mallows weights}
\label{sec:mallowsEfficiency}

\script{mallowsEfficiency.R} quantifies the price of the
leverage protection at the clean Gaussian model across the tuning grid
($\gamma \in \{1, 2\}$, cut-off $\in \{0.95, 0.975, 0.99\}$), reporting
the relative efficiency of $\hat\beta$ against \code{lmer} (the MLE)
and against the plain RSE. \script{mallowsEfficiencyDim.R}
repeats this as the number of continuous covariates grows, to check
that the $\chi^2$-calibrated cut-off keeps the false-down-weighting
rate roughly constant with dimension.

\emph{Conclusions.} At the default tuning ($\gamma = 1$, cut-off
$0.975$) the clean-model cost is about $\Sexpr{fmt(nums$mallows$costVsRsePct, 1)}\%$
in the variance of $\hat\beta$ relative to the plain RSE (over
$\Sexpr{nums$mallows$nreps}$ replicates), with only about
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$mallows$pctDown, 1)}\%$ of clean observations
down-weighted; the cost is stable as the covariate dimension grows and
vanishes for designs without continuous covariates. Only the most
aggressive corner of the grid ($\gamma = 2$, cut-off $0.95$) costs
appreciably more, which is why the gentler default is shipped.

\subsection{RANSAC: basin radius and multi-start consensus}
\label{sec:ransacStudies}

\script{ransacBasin.R} separates the two failure modes the
\code{rlmer_ransac} diagnostics watch for: a \emph{fixed-effect basin}
condition (a redescending \code{rho.e} only keeps the high-breakdown
\textsc{ransac} start if the start lies within the bisquare
support-preservation radius $r^\ast$) and a distinct
\emph{random-effects} attractor in which the fitted RE correlation
collapses to a phony $|\hat\rho| \to 1$. \script{ransacConsensus.R}
then evaluates the multi-start consensus (\code{rlmer_ransac(n_starts =
  m)}) under several error/RE contamination generators.

\emph{Conclusions.} The basin radius is genuinely a fixed-effect
property and the phony-correlation failure is a separate random-effects
phenomenon, so the two are reported by separate diagnostics. Running
\code{rlmer} from the $\Sexpr{nums$ransacConsensus$nStarts}$ best
distinct \textsc{ransac} starts and keeping the interior,
lowest-residual-scale fit reduces the rate of phony $|\hat\rho| \to 1$
solutions relative to a single start, from
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$ransacConsensus$phonySingle, 3)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$ransacConsensus$phonyConsensus, 3)}$ pooled over the
$\Sexpr{nums$ransacConsensus$nGenerators}$ generators, at essentially
unchanged slope accuracy: it recovers the good solution in the
replicates where the single best start falls into the phony attractor.

\subsection{Structured random-effect covariances}
\label{sec:structuredCovariances}

\script{structuredCovariances.R} studies the new \code{cs} and
\code{ar1} covariance structures. Balanced repeated-measures datasets
(generated with \code{generateRepeatedMeasuresDatasets}) are fitted
classically (\code{lmer}) and robustly (\code{rlmer}) under both clean
data and random-effects-level contamination, and the recovered common
correlation and marginal standard deviations are compared with the
truth.

\emph{Conclusions.} On clean data both fits recover the truth, so the
projection-based fitting is consistent. Under contamination of
$\Sexpr{nums$structured$contamPct}\%$ of the subjects the classical
common correlation collapses toward zero (from a true
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$structured$cs$trueRho)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$structured$cs$contLmer)}$ for \code{cs} and from
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$structured$ar1$trueRho)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$structured$ar1$contLmer)}$ for \code{ar1}) and the
marginal standard deviations inflate, whereas the robust structured fit
stays near the truth (correlation
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$structured$cs$contRlmer)}$ and
$\Sexpr{fmt(nums$structured$ar1$contRlmer)}$). The \code{cs}/\code{ar1}
fitting is experimental (see \code{vignette("robustlmm-3.5.0")}).

\clearpage
\section{Studies for the small-sample and variance-component methods}
\label{sec:newMethods}

Version 3.5.1 makes the bias-reduced (CR2) cluster
sandwich with small-sample degrees of freedom the default reference of
\code{anova} and \code{confint} for \code{vcov\_type = "sandwich"}, sets
near-zero nuisance variance components to zero when generating the
bootstrap variance-component test, tests several added variance
components jointly with the robust score test, and ranks \textsc{ransac}
candidates by a centred residual scale. This section describes the
simulation studies behind these changes. Their scripts ship with the
package (in \code{system.file("simulationStudy", package =
"robustlmm")}); the numbers below are read from the results of runs with
the development version of \pkg{robustlmm} 3.5.1. A decision rule was
fixed before each study was run.

\subsection{Small-sample references for the cluster sandwich}
\label{sec:smallJSandwich}

Three designs, each with 10 observations per cluster unless stated
otherwise and 1000 replicates per setting. (i) A design like
\code{sleepstudy} (random intercept and slope), $J \in \{8, 12, 18,
30\}$, for the coverage of the $95\%$ intervals of the intercept and the
within-cluster slope (\script{t5\_smallJ\_sim.R}). (ii) Design A
adds three null terms to it: a between-cluster covariate, a
between-cluster factor with three levels and a within-cluster covariate,
$J \in \{8, 12, 18, 30\}$. (iii) Design B has unbalanced clusters of 4 to
16 observations, $t_4$ errors, a binary and a log-normal between-cluster
covariate, a within-cluster covariate and a joint test of the two
between-cluster covariates, $J \in \{10, 20, 40\}$. Designs A and B were
each run twice with independent seeds; Table~\ref{tab:smallJ} shows the
second run, which goes through \code{anova} and \code{confint}
(\script{f3d\_replication\_sim.R}; the first runs are
\script{f3b\_cr2\_sim.R} and \script{f3c\_confirm\_sim.R}).

\emph{Conclusions.} In design (i) the reference quantile is the main
problem: the G1 sandwich with normal quantiles covered the intercept in
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichWithin$covG1z[1, "int"], 3)}$ of the
replicates at $J = 8$, with $t_{J-1}$ quantiles in
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichWithin$covG1t[1, "int"], 3)}$. With
between-cluster terms the G1 sandwich itself is biased down and has far
fewer effective degrees of freedom than $J - 1$: the $\chi^2$ table
rejected between $\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$oldRange[1])}$ and
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$oldRange[2])}$ of the true null
hypotheses, and even with $t_{J-1}$ quantiles the intercept interval
covered only $\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$firstG1tIntMin)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$firstG1tIntMax)}$ in design A. The CR2
sandwich \citep{bell2002bias} with approximate-Hotelling degrees of
freedom \citep{pustejovsky2018small} is much closer to the nominal
level: at $J \ge 20$ its size was
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$newRangeJ20[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$newRangeJ20[2])}$
($\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$firstNewRangeJ20[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$firstNewRangeJ20[2])}$ in the first
runs) and its intervals covered
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$covRangeJ20[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$covRangeJ20[2])}$. It is not exact:
for between-cluster terms the size can stay somewhat above $5\%$ (up to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$sandwichBetween$newMax)}$ for the binary covariate at
$J = 10$). The acceptance band fixed before the first run ($[0.025,
0.065]$ from $J = 12$) was met by no candidate; CR2 became the default
because it was the closest to the nominal level in every setting and
stayed within $[0.02, 0.08]$ at $J \ge 20$ in both runs.

<<smallJ-table, results=tex, echo=FALSE>>=
tb <- mnums$sandwichBetween$table
cell <- function(d, J, t, col) {
    v <- tb[tb$design == d & tb$J == J & tb$term == t, col]
    if (length(v)) fmt(v) else ""
}
rowsFor <- function(d, terms) {
    Js <- sort(unique(tb$J[tb$design == d]))
    for (J in Js) {
        sizes <- unlist(lapply(terms, function(t)
            c(cell(d, J, t, "old"), cell(d, J, t, "new"))))
        cov <- c(cell(d, J, terms[1], "cov_int"),
                 cell(d, J, terms[1], "cov_slope"))
        cat(paste(c(J, sizes, cov), collapse = " & "), "\\\\\n")
    }
}
cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering\\small\n")
cat("\\begin{tabular}{r*{6}{c}cc}\n\\toprule\n")
cat("& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{between covariate} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{between factor}",
    "& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{within covariate} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{coverage} \\\\\n")
cat("$J$ & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & int. & slope \\\\\n\\midrule\n")
cat("\\multicolumn{9}{l}{\\emph{Design A}} \\\\\n")
rowsFor("A", c("w", "f", "x"))
cat("\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\n\\medskip\n")
cat("\\begin{tabular}{r*{8}{c}cc}\n\\toprule\n")
cat("& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{binary} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{log-normal}",
    "& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{within} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{joint (2 df)}",
    "& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{coverage} \\\\\n")
cat("$J$ & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & $\\chi^2$ & CR2 & int. & slope \\\\\n\\midrule\n")
cat("\\multicolumn{11}{l}{\\emph{Design B}} \\\\\n")
rowsFor("B", c("z", "u", "v", "zu"))
cat("\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n")
cat("\\caption{Size at the nominal $5\\%$ level of the robust Wald tests with",
    "the G1 sandwich and the $\\chi^2$ table ($\\chi^2$) and with the CR2",
    "sandwich and approximate-Hotelling degrees of freedom (CR2), and",
    "coverage of the $95\\%$ CR2 intervals for the intercept and slope;",
    mnums$sandwichBetween$nreps, "replicates per row.}\n")
cat("\\label{tab:smallJ}\n\\end{table}\n")
@

\subsection{Near-zero nuisance variance components in the bootstrap test}
\label{sec:bootThreshold}

\code{anova(fit0, fit1, test = "boot")} tests an added random slope
when \code{fit0} has a random intercept and a nuisance random slope
whose standard deviation is $0$ (on the boundary) or $0.5$; $J \in \{18,
50\}$, 8 observations per group,
$\Sexpr{mnums$bootThreshold$table$n[1]}$ replicates per setting with one
bootstrap draw from each generator, \code{fit0}'s raw estimates and the
thresholded ones (\script{t2\_threshold\_sim.R},
\script{t2\_recount.R}).

\emph{Conclusions.} Under the null the robust fit set the tested
variance to exactly zero in $\Sexpr{pct(mnums$bootThreshold$atomRange[1])}$
to $\Sexpr{pct(mnums$bootThreshold$atomRange[2], 1)}\%$ of the samples.
The quasi-deviance difference then sits at its atom and its p-value is 1
whichever generator is used: the test is conservative near the null
(size $\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$bootThreshold$sizeJ50[1], 3)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$bootThreshold$sizeJ50[2], 4)}$ at $J = 50$), and the
two generators gave the same size in
$\Sexpr{mnums$bootThreshold$nIdenticalTied}$ of the
$\Sexpr{mnums$bootThreshold$nCells}$ settings. The simulation therefore
cannot show a finite-sample benefit of the threshold. It is the default
because generating from a small positive estimate makes the bootstrap
inconsistent when the nuisance component is zero \citep{guedon2024}.
Rarely ($\Sexpr{mnums$bootThreshold$nNegative}$ of
$\Sexpr{format(mnums$bootThreshold$nValues, big.mark = ",")}$ statistics)
the larger robust fit ends in a worse local solution and the difference
is clearly negative; this counts as no evidence against the null.
\code{test = "score"} uses only the null fit and has no such atom.

\subsection{Several added variance components}
\label{sec:scoreMulti}

\code{anova(fit0, fit1, test = "score")} with two added random slopes and
a random intercept under the null; $J \in \{18, 50\}$, 8 observations
per group, $\Sexpr{mnums$scoreMulti$nreps}$ replicates per setting with
one bootstrap draw each (\script{t1\_score\_multi\_sim.R}). The
settings are the clean null, the null with 2 of 18 or 5 of 50 groups
shifted by 5 standard deviations (outside the tested directions), and an
alternative with slope standard deviations $0.5$.

\emph{Conclusions.} The size at $5\%$ was
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$scoreMulti$sizeClean[1], 3)}$ and
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$scoreMulti$sizeClean[2], 3)}$ on clean data and
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$scoreMulti$sizeContam[1], 3)}$ and
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$scoreMulti$sizeContam[2], 3)}$ with contaminated
groups ($J = 18$ and $50$), and the power
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$scoreMulti$power[1])}$ and
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$scoreMulti$power[2], 3)}$.

\subsection{Ranking RANSAC candidates}
\label{sec:ransacRanking}

(i) A random-intercept model with $J = 20$ groups of 10 observations and
$20\%$ of the observations shifted by 8 error standard deviations,
$\Sexpr{mnums$ransac$nreps}$ replicates: \code{ransac\_lme4} ranking by
the centred bisquare M-scale against \code{Qn}
(\script{t4\_centred\_scale\_sim.R}). (ii) The same design and one
with $10\%$ bad leverage points ($\Sexpr{mnums$ransac$nInvar}$
replicates each), and the six phony-correlation generators of
\script{ransacConsensus.R} ($\Sexpr{mnums$ransac$nBasin}$ replicates in
total), with five ranking criteria and a bisquare \code{rho.e}; the final
\code{rlmer} fit from each start is compared
(\script{f2\_rerun\_sim.R}). (iii) A balanced two-level
between-cluster factor with three of ten clusters of one arm shifted by
8, $\Sexpr{mnums$ransac$f1Nreps}$ replicates: the ranking scale's
constant $b = 1/2$ against the design-adaptive $b = 1/4$ reported by
\code{design\_breakdown} (\script{f1\_adaptive\_b\_sim.R}).

\emph{Conclusions.} The centred scale gives better starts: the median
intercept error of the start fell from
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$startIntMedQn)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$startIntMedCentred)}$ (on clean data its RMSE
was $\Sexpr{pct(mnums$ransac$cleanRmseRatio - 1)}\%$ larger), at the
price of a larger slope error of the start (RMSE
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$startSlopeRmseCentred)}$ against
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$startSlopeRmseQn)}$). The start did not change
the final fit: from every criterion's start the final fit equalled the
fit from \code{rlmer}'s default start in
$\Sexpr{pct(mnums$ransac$sameAsDefault[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{pct(mnums$ransac$sameAsDefault[2])}\%$ of the replicates with
outliers or leverage points. These runs use the residual-scale function
of \code{setting = "RSEa"}, whose scale equation breaks down there, so a
redescending \code{rho.e} accepts the outliers from any start. The rate
of phony $|\hat\rho| \to 1$ fits was
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$phonyRange[1], 3)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$phonyRange[2], 3)}$ for every criterion. The
design-adaptive constant made the start worse (RMSE of the factor
coefficient $\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$f1StartB25)}$ against
$\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$f1StartB50)}$), so \code{ransac\_lme4} keeps
$b = 1/2$. Scoring the candidates on marginal instead of conditional
residuals gave $\Sexpr{fmt(mnums$ransac$f1StartMarg)}$: with conditional
residuals a contaminated cluster inside the subsample is absorbed by its
own random effect. This is not yet used by \code{ransac\_lme4}.

\clearpage
\section{Studies for the high-breakdown configuration}
\label{sec:highBreakdown}

Version 3.5.1 adds an S-type scale function
(\code{makeSScalePsi}, \code{sScalePsi}), a high-breakdown start
(\code{init = "robust"}) and the configuration \code{setting = "RSEhb"}
that combines them with redescending $\psi$-functions. This section
describes the breakdown study behind them. Its scripts ship with the
package (in \code{system.file("simulationStudy", package =
"robustlmm")}); the numbers below are read from the results of runs with
the development version of \pkg{robustlmm} 3.5.1. All studies use $J = 20$ groups of 10 observations (unless stated
otherwise), $y = 2 + x + b_j + e$ with standard normal $x$, $b_j$ and $e$,
and four kinds of contamination: vertical outliers (a fraction of the
observations shifted by 8), outlying clusters (a fraction of the groups
shifted by 8), and bad leverage points near ($x \approx 4$) or far ($x
\approx 8$) from the data with $y \approx 0$. A fit is counted as broken
down when its intercept error (vertical outliers, clusters) or slope
error (leverage points) exceeds $0.5$.

\subsection{Breakdown of the default settings}
\label{sec:bdDefault}

The residual scale is estimated from $\sum_i \eta_i \tau_i^2 w(t_i)(t_i^2
- \kappa) = 0$. For the \code{psi2propII} weights of the default
settings $w(t)(t^2 - \kappa)$ increases to a finite limit $\chi(\infty)$,
and the equation breaks down at the fraction $\kappa/(\kappa +
\chi(\infty))$ of outliers: $\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$bp[["RSEn"]], 3)}$ for
\code{setting = "RSEn"} and $\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$bp[["RSEa"]], 3)}$ for
\code{"RSEa"}. \script{bd2\_sigma\_theory.R} solves the equation
with the location known, and \script{bd23\_map\_sim.R} maps the
breakdown of the current settings and of candidate fixes
($\Sexpr{hnums$map$nreps}$ samples per setting).

\emph{Conclusions.} The scale is inflated long before the equation breaks
down: with $20\%$ of the observations shifted by 8 the equation alone
gives $\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$map$sigmaKnownLoc[["RSEn"]], 1)}$ times the true
scale for \code{"RSEn"} and $\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$map$sigmaKnownLoc[["RSEa"]], 1)}$
for \code{"RSEa"} (beyond its breakdown point), and the fitted models had
a median residual-scale estimate of
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$map$medSigmaRSEn[1], 1)}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$map$medSigmaRSEn[2], 1)}$ and
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$map$medSigmaRSEa[1], 1)}$, respectively. A
redescending \code{rho.e} then no longer rejects the outliers, whatever
the start. The simple variance components obey the same equation at the
level of the random effects: outlying clusters are absorbed as large
random effects, and at $20\%$ vertical contamination the random-effect
standard deviation was estimated as exactly zero in
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$map$sdbZero[1])}$ to $\Sexpr{pct(hnums$map$sdbZero[2])}\%$
of the robust fits ($\Sexpr{pct(hnums$map$sdbZeroLmer)}\%$ for
\code{lmer}).

\subsection{Starting values}
\label{sec:bdStarts}

\script{bd5\_starts\_sim.R} compares starts for the fully
redescending fit (bisquare \code{rho.e} and \code{rho.b} with $c =
4.685$, S-type scales): the default \code{lmer} start, the
\code{ransac\_lme4} start, the screen of \code{init = "robust"} (the
observations an S-estimate of the fixed-effects part keeps, then
\code{lmer} on them), the same screen with an MM-estimate, and a start
from a robust decomposition of the S-residuals into within- and
between-group parts.

\emph{Conclusions.} Over the contaminated settings with at most $30\%$
contamination the final fit broke down in
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$starts[["s_scr"]])}\%$ of the samples from the
S-screen, against $\Sexpr{pct(hnums$starts[["lmer"]])}\%$ from the
default start and $\Sexpr{pct(hnums$starts[["ransac"]])}\%$ from the
\textsc{ransac} start, whose half-sample candidates are all contaminated;
the MM-screen was worse ($\Sexpr{pct(hnums$starts[["mm_scr"]])}\%$). The
decomposition start ($\Sexpr{pct(hnums$starts[["s_dec"]])}\%$) only
works for a random intercept, so the S-screen became \code{init =
"robust"}.

\subsection[The configuration setting = "RSEhb"]{The configuration \texorpdfstring{\code{setting = "RSEhb"}}{setting = "RSEhb"}}
\label{sec:bdRSEhb}

\code{setting = "RSEhb"} uses \code{sScalePsi} for the residual scale
and simple variance components, a bisquare \code{rho.b} for simple
random effects, the start \code{init = "robust"}, and a bisquare
\code{rho.e} with $c = 3.444$ ($85\%$ efficiency). Its breakdown was
mapped against the default setting with fresh seeds, both with the
default \code{method = "DAStau"} (Table~\ref{tab:rsehb}; script
\script{bd6b\_confirm\_sim.R}, re-run on the final code by
\script{bd6b2\_rerun\_sim.R}). Efficiency and inference were studied with 20
and 50 groups on clean data ($\Sexpr{hnums$inference$nrepsClean}$
samples) and with 50 groups under $10\%$ vertical outliers or outlying
clusters ($\Sexpr{hnums$inference$nrepsCont}$ samples;
\script{bd6c\_dastau\_sim.R}).

\emph{Conclusions.} Over the settings with at most $30\%$ contamination
the fixed effects broke down in
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$config$meanBroken[["RSEhb"]])}\%$ of the samples,
against $\Sexpr{pct(hnums$config$meanBroken[["RSEn"]])}\%$ for the
default ($\Sexpr{pct(hnums$config$cleanBroken[1])}\%$ of clean samples
cross the same threshold). Beyond that ($40\%$ vertical outliers or
outlying clusters, $20\%$ leverage points) it breaks down like the
default. At the central model the fixed effects keep
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effHB50[["int"]])}\%$ (intercept) and
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effHB50[["slope"]])}\%$ (slope) of the
efficiency of \code{lmer} with 50 groups and
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effHB20[["int"]])}\%$ and
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effHB20[["slope"]])}\%$ with 20 (the default
setting: $\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effRSEnInt[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effRSEnInt[2])}\%$ and
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effRSEnSlope[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$effRSEnSlope[2])}\%$). The CR2 sandwich
intervals (\code{confint(fit, vcov\_type = "sandwich")}) covered
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$inference$covCR2[1])}$ to
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$inference$covCR2[2])}$ in every setting, whereas the
model-based Satterthwaite interval of the intercept covered only
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$inference$covSattIntC8)}$ with outlying clusters. With
\code{method = "DASvar"} the residual scale was
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$inference$dasvarSigmaLow, 1)}\%$ smaller than with
\code{"DAStau"}.

The limits: the variance components are biased under contamination
(mean residual scale $\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$inference$meanSigmaC8)}$ and
random-effect standard deviation
$\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$inference$meanSdbC8)}$ with outlying clusters,
residual scale $\Sexpr{fmt(hnums$inference$meanSigmaV8)}$ with vertical
outliers; truth 1). At $20$ to $30\%$ contamination the random-effect
standard deviation was estimated as zero in up to
$\Sexpr{pct(hnums$config$sdbZeroMax)}\%$ of the samples; with $30\%$
vertical outliers these fits broke down more often
($\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroV30[["broken"]]}$ of
$\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroV30[["n"]]}$ against
$\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroV30[["brokenOther"]]}$ of
$\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroV30[["nOther"]]}$), with $30\%$ outlying
clusters less often ($\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroC30[["broken"]]}$ of
$\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroC30[["n"]]}$ against
$\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroC30[["brokenOther"]]}$ of
$\Sexpr{hnums$config$zeroC30[["nOther"]]}$). All of this evidence comes
from random-intercept models with one covariate; blocks of correlated
random effects keep the monotone multivariate \code{rho.b} and get no
protection against outlying clusters.

<<rsehb-table, results=tex, echo=FALSE>>=
tb <- hnums$config$table
lab <- c(V8 = "vertical outliers", C8 = "outlying clusters",
         L4 = "near leverage points", L8 = "far leverage points")
cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering\\small\n")
cat("\\begin{tabular}{lrccc}\n\\toprule \n")
cat("contamination & fraction & default & \\code{RSEhb} &",
    "\\code{RSEhb}, $\\hat\\sigma_b = 0$ \\\\\n\\midrule \n")
for (ty in c("V8", "C8", "L4", "L8")) {
    for (e in sort(unique(tb$eps[tb$type == ty]))) {
        d <- tb[tb$type == ty & tb$eps == e & tb$est == "RSEn", ]
        h <- tb[tb$type == ty & tb$eps == e & tb$est == "RSEhb", ]
        cat(paste(c(if (e == min(tb$eps[tb$type == ty])) lab[[ty]] else "",
                    paste0(pct(e), "\\%"), fmt(d$broken), fmt(h$broken),
                    fmt(h$sdbZero)), collapse = " & "), "\\\\\n")
    }
}
cat("\\bottomrule \n\\end{tabular}\n")
cat("\\caption{Fraction of fits whose fixed effects broke down, for the",
    "default setting and for \\code{setting = \"RSEhb\"}, and the fraction",
    "of \\code{RSEhb} fits with the random-effect standard deviation",
    "estimated as zero;", hnums$config$nreps, "samples per row.}\n")
cat("\\label{tab:rsehb}\n\\end{table}\n")
@

\clearpage
